<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=geodesic-dome">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Geodesic dome</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1926">1926</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A geodesic dome is a hemispherical thin-shell structure based on a geodesic polyhedron, whose rigid triangular elements distribute stress and let it carry heavy loads for its size. The first was designed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> by Walther Bauersfeld, chief engineer of Carl <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-zeiss-ag">Zeiss</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a>, for a planetarium to house his projector. A larger dome, &quot;The Wonder of Jena&quot;, opened to the public on 18 July 1926. Buckminster Fuller coined the term &quot;geodesic&quot; in 1948 and 1949 and received a US patent on 29 June 1954, which led to its popularisation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geodesic-dome.jpg" alt="Geodesic dome" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eberhard von Nellenburg · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mtl._Biosphere_in_Sept._2004.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1926</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Jena, Germany</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Walther Bauersfeld · Buckminster Fuller</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Buckminster Fuller is credited with the US popularisation of the idea and received the 1954 patent, but he was not the original inventor.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/dome">Dome<span>19,280–11,700 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=geodesic-dome">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
